# Architecture

`backend/` is an async Python 3.15 service on FastAPI{% if include_postgres %}, PostgreSQL{% endif %} and external HTTP APIs, with typed logs, a typer CLI and uv, run with Docker Compose. Each business domain is one package. Each external system has one thin layer. Each process (the API, a worker, a CLI command) opens its resources once and passes them down.

The layout follows the per-domain packages of [fastapi-best-practices](https://github.com/zhanymkanov/fastapi-best-practices), [Polar](https://github.com/polarsource/polar/tree/main/server/polar) and [Netflix Dispatch](https://github.com/Netflix/dispatch/tree/master/src/dispatch), with the repository and service layers of Cosmic Python ([repository](https://www.cosmicpython.com/book/chapter_02_repository.html), [service layer](https://www.cosmicpython.com/book/chapter_04_service_layer.html)).

## Layers

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    entry["Entry points: router.py, cli.py, worker loops"] --> service["service.py (the domain API)"]
    service --> other["lower domains' service.py and read types"]
{%- if include_postgres %}
    service --> repo["repository.py (Postgres), API clients"]
    repo --> infra["Infrastructure: app/infrastructure/ (postgres, rate_limit, &lt;name&gt;_api)"]
{%- else %}
    service --> infra["Infrastructure: app/infrastructure/ (rate_limit, &lt;name&gt;_api clients)"]
{%- endif %}
    lifespan["app/lifespan: Resources and wiring"] -.opens.-> infra
    lifespan -.passes Resources and services.-> entry
```

1. Infrastructure, bound to no domain: {% if include_postgres %}the database layer, {% endif %}the rate limiter, and one package per external API (`app/infrastructure/<name>_api/`).
2. `app/shared/` holds domain-free building blocks: the `AppError` base, the error envelope, logging setup, request helpers{% if include_postgres %}, the named blocking executor{% endif %}. `app/configuration.py` beside it holds the app-wide settings.
3. Domain packages, one per business concept, under `app/{{ root_package }}/`.
4. `app/lifespan/` holds `Resources`, one lifespan per process role, and the wiring between domains.
5. Entry points only translate input and output: HTTP routers, CLI commands, worker loops.

```
app/
  main.py cli.py   process entry points
  lifespan/        Resources, one lifespan per process role, composition
  shared/          domain-free building blocks
  infrastructure/  {% if include_postgres %}postgres/ {% endif %}rate_limit/ <name>_api/
  {{ root_package }}/  one package per domain{% if include_accounts %}: accounts/ ...{% endif %}
```

Packages under `infrastructure/` and `{{ root_package }}/` are flat siblings, never nested. A big domain splits into sibling packages. A domain's `router.py` and `cli.py` sit beside its `service.py`, so one feature lives in one folder.

Dependencies point down only. A domain never imports a router, and infrastructure never imports a domain. A domain may import a lower domain's service and read types, never in a cycle. import-linter (`lint-imports`) enforces this with the contracts in `[tool.importlinter]` in `backend/pyproject.toml`: `layers` over entry points, middlewares, lifespan, domains, infrastructure and shared, and `acyclic_siblings` over `app.{{ root_package }}`{% if include_accounts %}. Two `forbidden` contracts keep routers, dependencies, CLI commands and entry modules away from every `models` and `repository` module, and keep models away from FastAPI and Starlette. One `protected` contract per domain lets only that domain import its models and repository{% endif %}. Each contract's `broken_contract_guidance` says how to fix a break.

## Data stores
{% if include_postgres %}
PostgreSQL is the only store and the source of truth.

A domain owns its tables. Other domains read them through its service, never with their own SQL. A reference to another domain's row is an id, never an ORM relationship.
{%- else %}
The app keeps no state of its own. It has no database, and request budgets live in the API process's memory, so a restart clears them.
{%- endif %}

## A domain package

```
app/{{ root_package }}/<domain>/
{%- if include_postgres %}
  models.py          SQLAlchemy models for the tables this domain owns
{%- endif %}
  schemas.py         Pydantic request and response bodies, only when the domain has a router
  types.py           read types other domains may use (frozen models)
  enums.py           closed value sets shared with other domains
  errors.py          AppError subclasses, only when callers need to tell them apart
  service.py         <Domain>Service: use cases, the only thing other code calls
{%- if include_postgres %}
  repository.py      queries for this domain's own tables, no decisions
{%- endif %}
  router.py          HTTP routes, thin
  dependencies.py    request-scoped injection, only when a router needs it
  sweeps.py          clock-driven passes a worker loop runs, only when the domain has any
  cli.py             CLI commands for this domain, only when it has any
  configuration.py   pydantic-settings for this domain, only when it has settings
  log_events.py      typed log records
```

A file exists only when something runs, opens, injects, configures or raises through it. There is no catch-all `adapters.py`, `utils.py` or `helpers.py`. A file is named after what it implements.

- The service holds the use cases and is the domain's public API. Code outside the domain calls `service.py`{% if include_postgres %}, never the repository, so storage can move without touching callers{% endif %}. The service calls {% if include_postgres %}its own repository, {% endif %}API clients and lower domains' services. A big service splits by use case into `<feature>_service.py` files in the same domain, never into a new layer.
{%- if include_postgres %}
- The repository is data access for the domain's own tables (`find_account()`, `insert_session()`). It runs queries and decides nothing.
{%- endif %}
- Other domains read a domain's data through a read type from `types.py`.{% if include_postgres %} The ORM model stays inside its domain.{% endif %}
- The package that owns the {% if include_postgres %}table or the {% endif %}API owns the model. No other package redefines it.
- The service gets what it needs through its constructor, by keyword{% if include_accounts %} (`AccountService(repository=..., relying_party=...)`){% endif %}. It never reads `app.state`, module globals or `Resources`, so it runs the same in a request, a worker loop and a CLI command. A `classmethod`{% if include_postgres %} such as `on_session(session)`{% endif %} builds it for the common case.
- A service returns typed models. A tuple or dict return is a missing model.
- The service owns domain decisions. Routers and CLI commands parse input, call one service method, and shape the output or error.

### Calling another domain

Down the graph, a service takes the lower domain's service through its constructor. The wiring is written once, in `app/lifespan/`, and routers and CLI commands take services from there.

Up the graph, the contracts refuse it. Two domains that need each other share a third, higher domain that owns the orchestration.

{%- if include_postgres %}

## Postgres

`app/infrastructure/postgres/` holds `Database` (async engine and session factory, with statement, lock and idle-in-transaction timeouts per connection), the ORM `Base`, the `SurrogateId` and `Timestamps` mixins (a `bigint` identity id, and `created_at` and `updated_at` as `timestamptz`) and `build_postgres_enum`.

A repository takes an `AsyncSession` by keyword and holds no business rules:

```python
class AccountRepository:
    def __init__(self, *, session: AsyncSession) -> None:
        self.session = session

    async def find_account(self, account_id: int) -> Account | None:
        return await self.session.get(Account, account_id)
```

Partial reads use `select()` with explicit columns, locked reads `with_for_update()`, and bulk writes `insert().values([...])`. SQL is never string-formatted.

### Database roles

The postgres image makes `POSTGRES_USER` a superuser, so the app has two roles.

`{{ database_name }}` is that superuser and owns every object. Only `migrate`, the backup scripts and the operator connect as it.

`{{ database_name }}_application` is the role of the API and any worker. It is `NOSUPERUSER NOCREATEDB NOCREATEROLE NOREPLICATION NOBYPASSRLS`, with `CONNECT` on the database, `USAGE` on schema `public`, `SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE` on its tables and `USAGE, SELECT` on its sequences. It can't create, alter or drop anything, because PostgreSQL 15 and later keep `CREATE` on `public` from `PUBLIC` ([schemas](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-schemas.html#DDL-SCHEMAS-PATTERNS)) and schemas carry no other default grants ([privileges](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-priv.html)). Without superuser it can't run `COPY ... TO PROGRAM` or read server files.

Alembic revision `0003` creates the role when it is missing and grants on the existing tables and sequences, `alembic_version` included, which the API's schema check reads. `ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES FOR ROLE {{ database_name }}` covers the tables and sequences later revisions create, and only those ([ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-alterdefaultprivileges.html)), which is why the existing ones get their own `GRANT`. A revision that creates something in another schema, or a function the API calls, grants on it itself.

The role lives in a revision, not a `/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d` script, because that script runs only on an empty data directory, so an app updated over an existing volume would never get the role. Roles are cluster-wide, so a WAL-G restore brings the role back with the base backup. A revision can't hold a password, so `migrations/env.py` sets it after every upgrade from `{{ environment_prefix }}_APPLICATION_DATABASE_PASSWORD`. It hashes the password to a SCRAM verifier on the client (psycopg's `pgconn.encrypt_password`) and sends only the verifier, so the password never reaches a server log or a command line. Rotating it is a change to the API's env file and a deploy.

### Units of work

A session is one transaction and one unit of work, such as a request, a worker pass or a CLI command. The entry point opens it and commits or rolls back (`DatabaseSession` in `app/lifespan/dependencies.py` for a request). Services and repositories only `flush`, so two repositories called by one service write atomically. A transaction never spans two databases.

A write that must be serialised across processes takes `pg_advisory_xact_lock` on a named key at the start of its transaction, so the lock ends with the transaction.

### Pool budget

Each process holds its own pool. The sum over every process stays under `max_connections` (100) minus `superuser_reserved_connections` (3). The API's pool is `API_DATABASE_POOL_SIZE` 10 plus `API_DATABASE_MAXIMUM_OVERFLOW` 5, at most 15 (`app/lifespan/composition.py`). Count every other process at its maximum: each worker's pool, the `migrate` run, a CLI command, and the backup scripts' `psql` and `wal-g` sessions. The API's role is no superuser, so a runaway pool can't take the reserved slots. Shrink a pool before raising `max_connections`, because the server sizes shared memory by it and every connection can claim its own `work_mem`.

## Postgres operations

### Server settings

The `postgres` service passes every setting as a `-c` flag in `docker-compose.yml`, so every environment runs the same values and `SHOW <setting>` proves what is live.

| Setting | Value | PG18 default | Why |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `shared_preload_libraries` | `pg_stat_statements` | empty | The module needs shared memory, so it loads at start ([pg_stat_statements](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/pgstatstatements.html)). `compute_query_id` stays `auto`, which the module turns on ([statistics](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-statistics.html#GUC-COMPUTE-QUERY-ID)). Revision `0002` creates the extension, because the view exists only in a database that created it. |
| `max_connections` | `100` | typically 100 | Explicit for the pool budget. A change needs a restart ([connections](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-connection.html#GUC-MAX-CONNECTIONS)). |
| `shared_buffers` | `POSTGRES_SHARED_BUFFERS`, default `256MB` | `128MB` | A quarter of the production container's 1 GB limit, the manual's starting point ([resources](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-resource.html#GUC-SHARED-BUFFERS)). |
| `effective_cache_size` | `POSTGRES_EFFECTIVE_CACHE_SIZE`, default `768MB` | `4GB` | A planner estimate that allocates nothing ([query planning](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-query.html#GUC-EFFECTIVE-CACHE-SIZE)). The default assumes more memory than the container has, so it is the limit minus `shared_buffers`. |
| `work_mem` | `POSTGRES_WORK_MEM`, default `8MB` | `4MB` | Per sort or hash operation, before spilling to temp files. One query can run several, and hash operations get `hash_mem_multiplier` (2.0) times as much ([resources](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-resource.html#GUC-WORK-MEM)). |
| `maintenance_work_mem` | `POSTGRES_MAINTENANCE_WORK_MEM`, default `64MB` | `64MB` | Also each autovacuum worker's budget, because `autovacuum_work_mem` is -1 ([resources](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-resource.html#GUC-MAINTENANCE-WORK-MEM)). Explicit so it moves with the other memory settings. |
| `log_min_duration_statement` | `500ms` | `-1` (off) | The slow-query log ([logging](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html#GUC-LOG-MIN-DURATION-STATEMENT)). |
| `log_parameter_max_length` | `0` | `-1` (in full) | Keeps bind parameters, and so users' values, out of slow-query lines ([logging](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html#GUC-LOG-PARAMETER-MAX-LENGTH)). |
| `log_lock_waits` | `on` | `off` | Logs a lock wait longer than `deadlock_timeout` (1 s) ([logging](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html#GUC-LOG-LOCK-WAITS), [locks](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-locks.html#GUC-DEADLOCK-TIMEOUT)). |
| `log_temp_files` | `0` | `-1` (off) | Logs every temporary file with its size. A spill means a query outgrew `work_mem` ([logging](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html#GUC-LOG-TEMP-FILES)). |
| `log_autovacuum_min_duration` | `1s` | `10min` | Logs autovacuum actions of 1 s or more, and any skipped for a lock ([logging](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html#GUC-LOG-AUTOVACUUM-MIN-DURATION)). |
| `log_line_prefix` | `%m [%p] %q%u@%d app=%a sqlstate=%e query_id=%Q ` | `%m [%p] ` | Adds user, database, application name (`<service name>-api` for the API), SQLSTATE and the query id, which matches `pg_stat_statements.queryid`. `%q` ends the prefix for background processes ([logging](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html#GUC-LOG-LINE-PREFIX)). |
| `archive_mode` | `POSTGRES_ARCHIVE_MODE`, `off` locally, `on` in production | `off` | `deploy/deploy.sh` and the backup scripts export `POSTGRES_ARCHIVE_MODE=on`. Local and e2e stacks have nowhere to archive to. |
| `archive_timeout` | `60` | off | Switches segments after a minute with activity, so a lost disk loses about a minute ([WAL](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-wal.html#GUC-ARCHIVE-TIMEOUT)). |
| `archive_command`, `restore_command` | `wal-g wal-push %p`, `wal-g wal-fetch %f %p` | empty | Run only while archiving and during recovery. The local image has no `wal-g` and never calls it. |

`shm_size: 128mb` gives parallel queries room in `/dev/shm`, as in the postgres image's own compose example ([postgres image](https://hub.docker.com/_/postgres)).

`docker compose logs postgres` shows the slow-query, lock-wait, temp-file and autovacuum lines. `pg_stat_statements` ranks statements by total time:

```sql
SELECT query, calls, total_exec_time, mean_exec_time FROM pg_stat_statements ORDER BY total_exec_time DESC LIMIT 5;
```

### Memory limits

Docker sets no memory limit by default ([resource constraints](https://docs.docker.com/engine/containers/resource_constraints/)), so `deploy/docker-compose.production.yml` sets `mem_limit` on every service: 512 MB for `api` and `migrate`, and `POSTGRES_MEMORY_LIMIT` (default `1g`) for `postgres`. The Postgres limit holds:

- `shared_buffers`, 256 MB, allocated at start
- `/dev/shm`, up to 128 MB, which counts against the container like any tmpfs
- three autovacuum workers (the default `autovacuum_max_workers`, [autovacuum](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-autovacuum.html#GUC-AUTOVACUUM-MAX-WORKERS)) at `maintenance_work_mem` each, 192 MB
- the API's 15 connections with one or two `work_mem` operations each, about 240 MB

That is about 816 MB. The rest is headroom for connection overhead and the page cache, which the kernel shrinks before it kills anything. To grow, raise `POSTGRES_MEMORY_LIMIT` and `POSTGRES_SHARED_BUFFERS` together so `shared_buffers` stays a quarter of the limit, and set `POSTGRES_EFFECTIVE_CACHE_SIZE` to the limit minus `shared_buffers`.

### Backups

Production archives every WAL segment and takes a base backup each day with [WAL-G](https://github.com/wal-g/wal-g), into an S3-compatible bucket. A restore replays the newest base backup plus the archived WAL up to the last archived minute. A scheduled `pg_dump` can't replace it, because dumps can't take part in continuous archiving ([continuous archiving](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/continuous-archiving.html), [pg_dump](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/app-pgdump.html)). WAL-G pushes each segment from `archive_command`, takes base backups, encrypts on the client with libsodium, and talks to any store that takes `AWS_ENDPOINT` and path-style addressing ([storages](https://github.com/wal-g/wal-g/blob/v3.0.9/docs/STORAGES.md)).

- `deploy/postgres/Dockerfile` is `postgres:18-alpine`, the same base as local runs so the data volume and its collation carry over, plus WAL-G built from the `v3.0.9` tag and checked against its commit. It builds WAL-G with `-tags libsodium` because WAL-G's releases are glibc builds and Alpine packages none ([building](https://github.com/wal-g/wal-g/blob/v3.0.9/docs/README.md)).
- `deploy/postgres/backup.env.example` lists the backup keys. The real file lives outside the checkout with mode 600, and `{{ environment_prefix }}_POSTGRES_BACKUP_ENVIRONMENT_FILE` names it. Compose hands it to the production `postgres` container as `env_file:`, because `archive_command` runs there. `docker inspect` can read it, which already takes root on the host. No credential is ever a command argument.
- `WALG_LIBSODIUM_KEY` is 32 random bytes in hex. Write it straight into the file with `printf 'WALG_LIBSODIUM_KEY=%s\n' "$(openssl rand -hex 32)" >> <file>` and keep a copy off the host and away from the bucket. Without it no backup can be read. Old backups need the old key, so it can't be replaced in place.
- The bucket credentials get read, write and list. Nothing here deletes, so retention is a lifecycle rule on the bucket, longer than the restore window you promise. Expiring by age is safe because a base backup lands every day. `WALG_PREVENT_WAL_OVERWRITE` makes a retried push compare with the archived segment instead of overwriting it ([PostgreSQL](https://github.com/wal-g/wal-g/blob/v3.0.9/docs/PostgreSQL.md)), so a bucket with object lock works too.
- `deploy/postgres/backup_postgres.sh` checks that `archive_mode` is on, writes a restore point, switches the WAL segment and waits until `pg_stat_archiver` reports it archived. Then it runs `wal-g backup-push` and `wal-g wal-verify integrity timeline`, and fails on any lost segment. `deploy/deploy.sh` runs it after every deploy, so every migration has a fresh base backup behind it.
- `deploy/postgres/verify_postgres_backup_restore.sh` is the restore drill. It refuses when the newest base backup is older than `POSTGRES_RESTORE_DRILL_MAXIMUM_BASE_BACKUP_AGE_HOURS` (default 26). It counts every table's rows in production, switches the WAL segment and waits for the archive. Then it starts a throwaway container from the production image on `backend_egress` with no published port, runs `wal-g backup-fetch LATEST`, replays the archived WAL with `recovery.signal` and `restore_command`, and promotes ([recovery](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/continuous-archiving.html#BACKUP-PITR-RECOVERY)). The copy runs with `archive_mode=off`, so it never pushes into the production prefix. It passes when the copy is out of recovery, its Alembic revision equals production's, and every row count is within `POSTGRES_RESTORE_DRILL_ROW_SLACK` (default 10) of production's. It prints the seconds to a usable database and the last replayed commit, then removes the container and its volume. Production gets only reads, one restore point and one WAL switch.

Restore after losing the host: put back the checkout, the deploy `.env`, the database owner env file and the backup env file with the key, then run

```sh
export {{ environment_prefix }}_ENVIRONMENT_FILE=<deploy .env> {{ environment_prefix }}_DATABASE_OWNER_ENVIRONMENT_FILE=<database owner env file> \
  {{ environment_prefix }}_POSTGRES_BACKUP_ENVIRONMENT_FILE=<backup env file> POSTGRES_ARCHIVE_MODE=on
compose=(docker compose --env-file "${{ environment_prefix }}_ENVIRONMENT_FILE" -f docker-compose.yml -f deploy/docker-compose.production.yml)
"${compose[@]}" build postgres
"${compose[@]}" run --rm --no-deps --user postgres --entrypoint sh postgres -c \
  'mkdir -p -m 0700 "$PGDATA" && wal-g backup-fetch "$PGDATA" LATEST && touch "$PGDATA/recovery.signal"'
deploy/deploy.sh
```

Postgres replays every archived segment, then promotes to a new timeline. A long replay can outlast `--wait-timeout`. When `deploy.sh` gives up, wait for "archive recovery complete" in `docker compose logs postgres` and run it again.

### Backup schedule

WAL archiving runs inside Postgres. Base backups and drills run from `deploy/postgres/run_postgres_backup_schedule.sh`, a plain loop with no cron entry or systemd timer. It takes a base backup every `POSTGRES_BACKUP_INTERVAL_SECONDS` (default a day), runs the drill every `POSTGRES_RESTORE_DRILL_INTERVAL_SECONDS` (default a week), retries after `POSTGRES_BACKUP_RETRY_SECONDS` (default an hour) when either fails, and prints one UTC-stamped line per run. Start it in its own tmux server so it outlives the login shell:

```sh
systemd-run --scope --unit=tmux-{{ project_slug }} tmux -L {{ project_slug }} new-session -d -s postgres-backups \
  -e {{ environment_prefix }}_ENVIRONMENT_FILE=<deploy .env> -e {{ environment_prefix }}_DATABASE_OWNER_ENVIRONMENT_FILE=<database owner env file> \
  -e {{ environment_prefix }}_POSTGRES_BACKUP_ENVIRONMENT_FILE=<backup env file> \
  "deploy/postgres/run_postgres_backup_schedule.sh 2>&1 | tee -a <log file>"
```

Both scripts take a lock, so a run by hand and the loop never overlap. A reboot ends the loop. Its first pass after a start runs at once, so it catches up. While it is down, a drill run by hand fails on the base backup's age, and each deploy still takes a base backup.
{%- endif %}

## Connection packages and external HTTP APIs

A connection package owns one external system's configuration and client:

```
app/infrastructure/<name>_api/
  configuration.py   base URL, key or credentials (SecretStr), timeouts
  client.py          <Name>ApiClient: one method per endpoint, typed arguments, returns models
  schemas.py         request and response bodies as Pydantic models
  errors.py          <Name>ApiError and the few cases callers handle (NotFound, RateLimited)
```

- httpx is the one HTTP client. Each API gets one `httpx.AsyncClient` per process, opened on the process's `AsyncExitStack`, with `base_url`, default headers and an explicit `httpx.Timeout`.
- The client method is the contract. It maps statuses to exceptions and returns `Model.model_validate(response.json())`. Callers never see `httpx.Response` or raw dicts.
- Retries use [stamina](https://stamina.hynek.me/en/stable/), only on connection errors, 429 and 5xx, only for requests safe to repeat, and honour `Retry-After`. `httpx.AsyncHTTPTransport(retries=n)` retries only connect failures ([transports](https://www.python-httpx.org/advanced/transports/)).
- Credentials live in the client, never in a caller. A static key is a default header. An expiring token is refreshed by one shared task, so parallel callers never refresh twice ([RFC 9700 §4.14.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9700#section-4.14.2)).
- When an optional integration is down, the request still works and the failure is logged.
- Any retry, not only an HTTP one, is bounded, backs off with jitter, and covers only errors a retry can fix.
- An API package's bodies go in `schemas.py`, because `schemas.py` means Pydantic wire shapes everywhere{% if include_postgres %} and `models.py` means SQLAlchemy tables{% endif %}.

## Configuration

Every setting lives in a pydantic-settings `BaseSettings` in a `configuration.py`, one per package that has settings, each with its own `env_prefix`: `{{ environment_prefix }}_` for the app and `{{ environment_prefix }}_<PACKAGE>_` for a package{% if include_accounts %}, such as `{{ environment_prefix }}_ACCOUNTS_`{% endif %}. App-wide settings (service identity, {% if include_postgres %}database host, port and passwords, {% endif %}trusted proxy addresses, log level) go in `app/configuration.py`.

- Non-secret settings carry their default in the class. The environment only overrides it.
- Secrets have no default and are `SecretStr`{% if include_postgres %} with `repr=False`{% endif %}, so a missing one fails at startup.
{%- if include_postgres %}
- No env file holds a DSN. `ConnectionSettings.build_application_database_url()` and `MigrationConnectionSettings.build_migration_database_url()` build it with `PostgresDsn.build` from the password, the role and database names, the `postgresql+psycopg` driver, `{{ environment_prefix }}_DATABASE_HOST` (default `postgres`) and `{{ environment_prefix }}_DATABASE_PORT` (default 5432), and return a `SecretStr`. The password is percent-encoded, so any character works.
{%- endif %}
- Each settings getter is `@lru_cache` ([FastAPI settings](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/advanced/settings/#creating-the-settings-only-once-with-lru-cache)).
- Services get settings values by keyword from the lifespan or a dependency{% if include_accounts %} (`AccountService.on_session(session, relying_party=...)`){% endif %} and never read settings themselves.
- Nothing else reads the environment. ruff's banned-api refuses `os.environ` and `os.getenv`{% if include_postgres %}, and `migrations/env.py` builds its DSN from `read_migration_connection_settings()`{% endif %}.
{%- if include_postgres %}
- The example env files list every key. Compose's `environment:` never repeats one. Each secret reaches only the containers that use it:
  - `.env.example` becomes the API's env file (`{{ environment_prefix }}_ENVIRONMENT_FILE`, default `.env`), handed to `api` and `migrate` through `env_file:`. It holds `{{ environment_prefix }}_APPLICATION_DATABASE_PASSWORD`, the application role's password (`ConnectionSettings`), and also feeds compose interpolation.
  - `.env.database-owner.example` becomes the database owner env file (`{{ environment_prefix }}_DATABASE_OWNER_ENVIRONMENT_FILE`, default `.env.database-owner`), handed only to `postgres` and `migrate`. It holds `POSTGRES_PASSWORD`, read by the image and by `migrate` (`MigrationConnectionSettings`). `migrate` also reads the application role's password from the API's env file to set it. The API never gets the superuser password.
{%- else %}
- `.env.example` lists every key. Compose hands the env file (`{{ environment_prefix }}_ENVIRONMENT_FILE`, default `.env`) to the backend containers through `env_file:`, and its `environment:` never repeats a key.
{%- endif %}

## Resources

`app/lifespan/resources.py` holds `Resources`, a frozen typed model. Each process role opens it once on an `AsyncExitStack` (`app/lifespan/composition.py`), so resources close in reverse order even when startup fails halfway. A missing or wrong resource fails at startup, before the first request{% if include_postgres %}, and the database check refuses a schema behind Alembic head{% endif %}. Dependencies read it through `ResourcesDependency`. Nothing else reads `app.state`.

## Startup does no work

`create_app` is a uvicorn factory (`factory=True`, started by `python -m app serve-api`), so importing `app.main` reads no settings. The API lifespan opens connections and returns. {% if include_postgres %}Migrations (the compose `migrate` service), seeding, rebuilding derived state and maintenance are deploy steps or worker passes{% else %}Seeding, rebuilding derived state and maintenance are deploy steps or worker passes{% endif %}, so a restart is fast and the same everywhere.

## Background work

Work outside a request runs in a worker process, never in the web server. The template ships no worker. An app that needs one adds `python -m app run-worker` as its own compose service from the same image. The worker opens `Resources` like the API and runs each job as a plain `async` loop of one pass{% if include_postgres %} in one transaction{% endif %} and a sleep, all in one `asyncio.TaskGroup`. A failed pass is logged and the loop goes on. A loop that dies is logged and ends the process, so compose restarts it.

- Every pass is idempotent and runs under a timeout. It can run twice or die halfway without damage. A pass that sends something records the send first.
{%- if include_postgres %}
- One worker is active. It takes a Postgres session advisory lock (`pg_try_advisory_lock`) at startup, and a second worker waits until the lock frees.
{%- else %}
- One worker is active. Compose runs one replica of the worker service.
{%- endif %}
- A pass loads what it needs each time, so a restarted worker picks up where {% if include_postgres %}the database says{% else %}the systems it reads say{% endif %}.
- SIGTERM cancels the loops, {% if include_postgres %}the passes roll back, {% endif %}and `Resources` closes. `stop_grace_period` is longer than the slowest pass.

{%- if include_postgres %}

There is no job queue library. Work deferred from a request is a row the request writes in its own transaction, and a worker loop picks it up, so the work exists only if the write committed.

## Events and webhooks

A change and the message about it use a transactional outbox. The event is written in the same transaction as the change and a worker sends it, so the message goes out if and only if the change commits ([transactional outbox](https://microservices.io/patterns/data/transactional-outbox.html)). The consumer must be idempotent.

A received webhook has its signature checked against the raw body, and old timestamps are rejected ([Standard Webhooks](https://www.standardwebhooks.com/)). Store the event id and skip repeats, record the work and return `2xx` at once, and never rely on arrival order.
{%- endif %}

## CLI

One entry point, `python -m app` (and `{{ project_slug }}` in `[project.scripts]`), built on typer. Each process role is a command: `serve-api`, and `run-worker` once there is a worker. A domain's commands live in its `cli.py` and are added to `app/cli.py`. A command opens only the resources it needs and calls a service.

{%- if include_postgres %}

## Migrations

Alembic owns the schema: one linear history in `backend/migrations/versions/` (`NNNN_<name>.py`), no `create_all`, no hand `ALTER` on a live database. `alembic check` needs a database, so it is no hook. After a model change, run `alembic revision --autogenerate` against a running stack, read the file, and `alembic check` must then report nothing.

An applied revision never changes. A fix is the next revision. `downgrade()` raises. `create index concurrently` goes in its own revision with `autocommit_block()`. A change to an existing column is expand, then contract once every caller has moved. A new or renamed Postgres enum member is a revision too (`ALTER TYPE ... ADD VALUE` or `RENAME VALUE`), and so is a Postgres extension, as `0002` creates `pg_stat_statements`.
{%- endif %}

## Verification

No unit tests. Verification is running the real system. The flows in `e2e/` start the compose stack and check it from outside. Day to day, the gates below run.

- A flow is one user journey through the public HTTP surface, start to finish, named `<journey>_flow.py`{% if include_accounts %}. `accounts_flow.py` signs up with a passkey, signs out and signs in again{% endif %}.
- No file per endpoint, operation or query, and no SQL fixture files. They pin the schema and the implementation, so every migration breaks them. An upsert, for example, is checked inside the journey that submits twice.
- Shared code lives in modules named by concern. `stack_commands.py` starts and stops the stack, `http_client.py` sends requests, and `flow_report.py` prints results{% if include_accounts %}. `software_passkey.py` signs like an authenticator{% endif %}. A flow never copies code from another flow.
- State comes in through the API, never through SQL inserts. Assertions go through the API too. A `psql` check through `docker compose exec` is allowed only for an invariant the API can't show, such as a row really deleted or a role lacking a privilege. It runs in a read-only transaction.
- Each flow starts its own stack under its own compose project, with {% if include_postgres %}fresh passwords for both database roles and {% endif %}a free local port. It prints one `PASS` or `FAIL` line per assertion and exits 0 only when all of them pass. It removes the stack and its volumes, also after a failure. Run output goes outside the repository.
- A flow is due after a change to its journey's behaviour, a migration, the compose files or the deploy files. Flows run by hand, never as a CI or hook gate.
- A flow that only pinned old details gets deleted or rewritten when a refactor breaks it.

## HTTP API conventions

- Every error leaves as `{"error": {"code", "message", "details", "parameters", "request_id"}}`. The last three appear only when they carry something. The status is the class and `code` is the cause, a stable flat `snake_case` name a client branches on, never renamed or reused. `message` is English for developers and a safe fallback. A client shows its own words, keyed by `code`.
- A refusal is an `AppError` subclass with a `code`, an HTTP status and a safe message. A value the message mentions also travels typed in `parameters`, a frozen `ErrorParameters` model the class names as `parameters_model`{% if include_accounts %} (`passkey_credential_id_too_long` carries `maximum_bytes`){% endif %}, so no client parses it out of English.
- No stack trace, SQL, exception text or upstream error text ever leaves. `app/shared/exception_handlers.py` and `app/unhandled_error_middleware.py` turn every exception into the envelope.
- Validation errors answer 400 `validation_error` with every field error in `details` as `{code, pointer}`. `pointer` is an [RFC 6901](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6901.html) JSON Pointer whose first segment names the location (`/body/credential/id`, `/query/limit`, `/path/account_id`). `code` is an `ErrorDetailCode` (`required`, `wrong_type`, `too_long` and the rest) mapped from pydantic's error type. The rejected input, pydantic's `ctx` and a validator's own text never leave. A body that is not JSON answers 400 `malformed_body`. Rate limits answer 429 `rate_limited` with `Retry-After`.
- Every response carries a server-minted `X-Request-ID`. A 5xx body repeats it as `request_id` so a user can quote it, and the server log holds the stack trace under the same id.
- The committed `backend/openapi.json` is the contract, and clients are built from it. `uv run python tools/dump_openapi.py openapi.json` rewrites it, so every API change shows in review as a diff of that file. `/docs` and `/openapi.json` are off, so the reference is published from the file. Each error status of an operation lists its codes in `x-error-codes`, and `x-error-parameters` gives the schema of each code that carries values. Both come from the `AppError` classes the route names in `responses=describe_error_responses((...))`, plus the shared `validation_error`, `malformed_body`, `rate_limited` and `internal_error`. `default` is the shared `ApiError` response, and every response documents `X-Request-ID`. A route that starts raising a new refusal names its class there. A new code on an existing operation is a contract change, so clients fall back on the status class for a code they don't know.
- Every route has a typed return annotation, which FastAPI uses as its response model, and no `response_model=` that repeats it (ruff `FAST`).
- Lists that grow paginate by cursor. Offset is only for small, bounded lists.
- POST routes a client may retry take idempotency keys.
- Payloads are minimal. A field has one name across REST, events and stored payloads.
- Request budgets are an in-memory GCRA in the API process (`app/infrastructure/rate_limit/`, `app/rate_limit_middleware.py`), per client IP. That allows one API replica only, because a second replica would double every budget.
- The client IP comes from `X-Real-IP` only when the peer is one of `{{ environment_prefix }}_TRUSTED_PROXY_ADDRESSES`.

## Python and async

Python 3.15 everywhere: `.python-version`, `requires-python`, ruff's `target-version`, ty and the Dockerfile `FROM` lines, bumped together in one change.

Every I/O path is `async`. A library that only blocks is wrapped once, in its infrastructure package, and runs on its own `BlockingExecutor`. Ruff's `ASYNC` rules catch blocking calls inside `async def`.

- Parallel calls go through `asyncio.TaskGroup`, which cancels the others when one fails. `asyncio.gather` is banned.
- Deadlines wrap the whole operation in `async with asyncio.timeout(...)`. `asyncio.wait_for` is banned.
- Fire-and-forget is only for best-effort work and keeps a reference to each task, because the event loop keeps only weak references ([asyncio tasks](https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio-task.html)).
- CPU-heavy work runs in a process pool or a worker, never on the event loop.

## Runtime defaults

- No custom JSON response class. With a typed return FastAPI serializes through Pydantic straight to bytes, and it deprecates `ORJSONResponse` and `UJSONResponse` ([custom response](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/advanced/custom-response/#orjson-or-response-model)). Both are banned.
- Other JSON goes through Pydantic's `model_validate_json`, `model_dump_json` and `TypeAdapter`. Raw JSON with no model uses [orjson](https://github.com/ijl/orjson) (`tools/dump_openapi.py`). The standard library `json` is banned.
- The event loop is [uvloop](https://pypi.org/project/uvloop/). uvicorn runs with `loop="uvloop"` (`app/cli.py`), and every other async entry point starts with `uvloop.run(main())`{% if include_postgres %} (`migrations/env.py`){% endif %}. Event-loop policies are deprecated since Python 3.14 ([asyncio policies](https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio-policy.html)), so `asyncio.run`, `asyncio.set_event_loop_policy` and `uvloop.install` are banned.
- Blocking work runs on a named `BlockingExecutor` per kind of work{% if include_postgres %} (`app/shared/blocking_executor.py`){% endif %}, opened on the process's `AsyncExitStack` and held on `Resources`{% if include_postgres %}. `file_read_executor` reads the Alembic head at startup{% else %}. The app has no blocking work, so it has no executor{% endif %}. `asyncio.to_thread` is banned, because the default executor also serves the loop's DNS lookups and one slow call there stalls name resolution for the whole process.
- `lazy import` ([PEP 810](https://peps.python.org/pep-0810/)) is for a heavy module a CLI command or worker only sometimes needs.

## Gates

`uvx prek run --all-files` runs every gate. [prek](https://github.com/j178/prek) reads `.pre-commit-config.yaml`, whose hooks run the tools locked in `backend/uv.lock` and `e2e/uv.lock`, plus a pinned gitleaks and a pinned `openapi-spec-validator`. Copier installs them as pre-commit and pre-push hooks (`uvx prek install`). "Banned" in this document means ruff's banned-api list (`[tool.ruff.lint.flake8-tidy-imports.banned-api]` in `backend/pyproject.toml`) refuses it.

- `uv.lock` is committed, and the hooks and the image install with `--locked`. `prerelease = "explicit"`. `exclude-newer = "7 days"` keeps releases younger than a week out of the lock, so a bad upload has time to be caught ([uv resolution](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/concepts/resolution/)).
- `ruff format` and `ruff check` run over `app tools{% if include_postgres %} migrations{% endif %}` with `select = ["ALL"]`, so dead code fails through `F401`, `F841` and `ARG`, and complexity through `C901` at `max-complexity = 10`, `PLR0911`, `PLR0912` and `PLR0915`. `D`, `CPY` and `COM812` are off. `TC001`, `TC002` and `TC003` stay off because FastAPI and Pydantic read annotations at runtime, so type-only imports break routes. Besides the bans named elsewhere here, banned-api refuses `typing.cast`, `typing.Any`, `requests`, `aiohttp` and `fastapi.HTTPException` (raise an `AppError` subclass).
- ty runs over `app tools`, pinned exactly because it is pre-1.0, with the possibly-* rules as errors and `respect-type-ignore-comments = false`.
- semgrep runs the structural rules ruff can't express (`semgrep/house-rules.yaml`, over `app`{% if include_postgres %} and `migrations`{% endif %}, pinned in `tools/run_house_rules.py`). The hook fails on any finding and on any parse error, because semgrep otherwise skips a file it can't parse, such as one with `lazy import`, without a word.
- lint-imports checks the contracts under Layers. A new domain takes its place in `layers`{% if include_accounts %} and adds its own `protected` contract{% endif %}.
- deptry checks that every dependency is used and every import is declared.
- The OpenAPI hook rewrites `backend/openapi.json` from the code and checks it as OpenAPI 3.1 with [openapi-spec-validator](https://github.com/python-openapi/openapi-spec-validator). A route change missing from the committed file fails the hook, because the hook leaves the file modified.
- `ruff format`, `ruff check` and `ty` also run over `e2e/`.
- gitleaks scans the git history.
- `uv audit` runs at pre-push and in the `manual` stage only, because it needs the network. It checks every locked backend dependency against the [OSV](https://osv.dev/) database and exits 1 on a known vulnerability and 2 when it can't reach the service. When a fix is younger than the 7-day `exclude-newer` cooldown, add an `exclude-newer-package` entry for that package in `backend/pyproject.toml`, run `uv lock --upgrade-package <name>`, and delete the entry once the cooldown has passed ([uv resolution](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/concepts/resolution/)). Run it by hand with `uvx prek run --hook-stage manual backend-audit`.

The shell scripts in `deploy/` have no hook. Run `shellcheck` on them after a change.

## Container image

`backend/Dockerfile` is multi-stage and follows [uv's Docker guide](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/guides/integration/docker/). Dependencies install before the source is copied, so a code change reuses the dependency layer. The virtualenv is built in a builder stage and copied whole, so a Python bump changes only the `FROM` lines. The image runs as a non-root user, and compose runs it read-only with a small `/tmp` tmpfs and every capability dropped. One image serves every process (`serve-api`{% if include_postgres %}, `alembic upgrade head`{% endif %}, a worker). `Dockerfile.dockerignore` keeps `.git`, `.venv`, `e2e/`, `deploy/`, env files and caches out of the build context.

## Environments

The app runs with Docker Compose on one host: `api`{% if include_postgres %}, `migrate` and `postgres`{% endif %}, behind a reverse proxy (`deploy/Caddyfile`). One image moves between environments, only environment variables differ, and every environment has its own {% if include_postgres %}database and {% endif %}credentials. `deploy/deploy.sh` builds the image from the checkout and starts the stack with `deploy/docker-compose.production.yml`, which joins the reverse proxy's network and sets `mem_limit` on every service. There is no rollback. A bad release is fixed forward.
{%- if include_postgres %}

In production the `postgres` service runs the image from `deploy/postgres/Dockerfile`, archives its WAL, reads the backup env file, joins `backend_egress` to reach the bucket, and gets 60 s to stop so the archiver can push what is left. `deploy/deploy.sh` needs `{{ environment_prefix }}_ENVIRONMENT_FILE`, `{{ environment_prefix }}_DATABASE_OWNER_ENVIRONMENT_FILE` and `{{ environment_prefix }}_POSTGRES_BACKUP_ENVIRONMENT_FILE` in its environment. It refuses to run when the database owner or backup env file is missing or not mode 600, and takes a base backup once the stack is up.
{%- endif %}

Every long-running service has:

- one health endpoint, `/health`, which returns 200 while the process runs and checks nothing else
- graceful shutdown, so on SIGTERM it stops taking work, finishes what is running, then closes resources, with a `stop_grace_period` longer than the slowest request or pass
- explicit maxima on pools, request body sizes (64 KB at the proxy) and request budgets

## Conventions

- Exceptions propagate. Catch only a known error you can name, and turn it into an `AppError` at the boundary. A raised error never copies exception text.
- A parameter is optional only when the domain has no value for it.
- No constants or wrappers without a second caller.
- Models we build go through keyword constructors, which ty checks. `model_validate` and `model_validate_json` read only data from outside (request bodies, external APIs), never our own rows or objects, so nothing uses `from_attributes`. JSON is never parsed with `isinstance` checks.
- The response bases are `strict=True`, because our own outputs are built with the declared types and a coerced value is a bug ([strict mode](https://pydantic.dev/docs/validation/latest/concepts/strict_mode/)).
- No `model_copy(update=...)`, because neither ty nor Pydantic checks the update dict. No call-site `include=` or `exclude=` on wire output. A different shape is a different model ([FastAPI response model](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/response-model/#response-model-include-and-response-model-exclude)).
- No type dispatch on our own classes. The behaviour lives on the class as a method or property, or Pydantic's discriminator picks the class ([Replace Conditional with Polymorphism](https://refactoring.com/catalog/replaceConditionalWithPolymorphism.html)). An enum's mapping is one property on the enum whose body is a `match` ending in `assert_never`, which ty proves exhaustive. `assert` is banned (ruff `S101`).
- Grouped values and collaborator carriers are frozen Pydantic models (`ConfigDict(frozen=True)`, plus `arbitrary_types_allowed=True` when fields are clients or services), like `Resources`. Dataclasses and NamedTuple bundles are banned.
- No underscore-private methods and no `@staticmethod`.
- Everything is UTC: timezone-aware `datetime`{% if include_postgres %}, `timestamptz` columns{% endif %}, `TZ=UTC` in every process.
- No comments or docstrings. A name, a type or a smaller function says it. The two exceptions are the reason on a linter suppression and the link to the upstream bug a workaround exists for.
- No hand-made decorators (`functools.wraps` is banned). The only decorators are the libraries' documented API: `router.get`, typer's `command`, `property`, Pydantic validators, `lru_cache` on settings getters.
- A change that introduces a pattern moves every caller in the same change.

## Logging

`configure_logging` in `app/shared/logging.py` sets up logging once, at the start of every process. Every logger comes from `app.shared.logging`.

- Output goes to unbuffered stdout, as JSON in containers and through a readable renderer on a terminal. Tracebacks render with quoted values redacted.
- Events are typed: `log.emit(logger, event=ApplicationStarted())`. Each event is a `LogEvent` model in the owning package's `log_events.py` with a fixed message and operation. Values go in fields, never in the message, and log context is bound only in `app/shared/logging.py`.
- The request's correlation id goes on every line of a request.
- Standard library logs go through the same processors, so a process writes one format.
- Secrets, tokens, full request or response bodies and personal data are never logged.
- The request log middleware writes one line per request.

## Naming

Code reads like a sentence at the call site: `await account_service.get_account_summary(account_id)`, not `await svc.get(a)`.

- A function takes at most one positional subject besides `self`. Everything after it is keyword-only (ruff `PLR0917` with `max-positional-args = 1`). Signatures a library fixes are the exception, each with a reason on the suppression.
- Names are long form, whole words: `configuration`, not `cfg`, and `error`, not `e`.
- A name doesn't repeat the type: `accounts`, not `account_list`.
- Functions are verb plus object and say what they return. `get_` returns one thing or raises, `find_` returns one thing or `None`, `list_` returns a list and `count_` an int. `insert_`, `delete_` and `send_` change something.
- Wire models put the thing first and the role last. A request body ends in `Request`. A response ends in `Response` or names its part, never `View`.
- Units go in the name when the type doesn't carry them: `TIMEOUT_SECONDS`, `max_size_bytes`, `created_at`.
- Constants are `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`. A constant shared by two domains belongs to the one that owns its meaning.
- Closed sets are enums, never a `Literal` with two or more string values.
- String identifiers are namespaced: log operations, env variables (`{{ environment_prefix }}_<SETTING>`), cookie names.

## Using the libraries fully

Before writing a helper, check whether the library already does it.

- FastAPI: dependencies as `Annotated` aliases, `fastapi.status` constants, `lifespan=`, one `APIRouter(prefix=..., tags=[...])` per domain.
- Pydantic: constraints in the type (`Field(gt=0, max_length=200)`, `SecretStr`), `ConfigDict(frozen=True)` for values that must not change, `TypeAdapter` for plain types, `JsonValue` for arbitrary JSON, `model_validate_json` and `model_dump(mode="json")` at the boundaries, discriminated unions instead of type checks.
- httpx: `base_url`, default headers, `httpx.Timeout`, `httpx.Auth`.
- Standard library: `enum.StrEnum`, `datetime.UTC`, `pathlib.Path`, `asyncio.TaskGroup` and `asyncio.timeout`, `itertools.batched`.

## Starting a new domain

{%- if include_postgres %}
1. Pick its place in the import-linter `layers` contract. It may import only what sits below it. Add its `protected` contract.
2. Create `app/{{ root_package }}/<domain>/models.py`, `repository.py`, `service.py`, and `types.py` if other domains read it.
3. Import its `models` in `migrations/env.py` and add an Alembic revision for its tables.
4. If it has settings, add `configuration.py` with its own `env_prefix`, and its keys in `.env.example`.
5. Add `router.py`, `schemas.py` and `dependencies.py` only when HTTP needs them, `sweeps.py` when a worker runs something for it, and `cli.py` when an operator needs it.
6. Add its wiring in `app/lifespan/`.
{%- else %}
1. Pick its place in the import-linter `layers` contract. It may import only what sits below it.
2. Create `app/{{ root_package }}/<domain>/service.py`, and `types.py` if other domains read it.
3. If it has settings, add `configuration.py` with its own `env_prefix`, and its keys in `.env.example`.
4. Add `router.py`, `schemas.py` and `dependencies.py` only when HTTP needs them, `sweeps.py` when a worker runs something for it, and `cli.py` when an operator needs it.
5. Add its wiring in `app/lifespan/`.
{%- endif %}
